<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-derivatives-recap" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>A Derivatives Refresher</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      This course is about <term>integral</term> calculus, but every technique
      in it leans on <term>differential</term> calculus you've already seen.
      Rather than assume that's all still fresh, we spend our first two class
      meetings reminding ourselves what a derivative actually is, and getting
      the rules for computing one back into your hands quickly.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-derivatives-refresher">
    <title>Derivative Rules and Critical Points</title>
    <!-- Sessions 1-2 . Tue Aug 25 & Thu Aug 27 -->

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-what-is-a-derivative">
      <title>What does a derivative actually mean?</title>

      <p>
        Before any rules: what problem is the derivative actually solving?
        Picture a curve <m>y = f(x)</m> and a point on it. You want to know
        how steep the curve is <em>right at that point</em> &#8212; not the
        average steepness between two far-apart points, but the steepness at
        one exact instant. That's the whole idea. Everything else is
        machinery for computing it.
      </p>

      <p>
        A first attempt: pick a second, nearby point on the curve and find
        the slope of the line connecting the two (a <term>secant line</term>).
        If the two points are <m>(x, f(x))</m> and <m>(x+h, f(x+h))</m>, that
        slope is
        <md>
          \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}.
        </md>
        This is only an <em>average</em> rate of change over the gap between
        the two points &#8212; not what we wanted. But if we let the gap
        <m>h</m> shrink toward <m>0</m>, the secant line swings around and
        settles onto the <term>tangent line</term> at <m>x</m>: the line that
        just grazes the curve at that single point. That limiting slope is
        the derivative.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-derivative">
        <title>The derivative</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <term>derivative</term> of <m>f</m> at <m>x</m>, written
            <m>f'(x)</m>, is
            <md>
              f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h},
            </md>
            provided the limit exists. Geometrically, <m>f'(x)</m> is the
            slope of the tangent line to <m>y=f(x)</m> at the point <m>x</m>.
            Just as often, we read it as a <term>rate of change</term>: how
            fast <m>f</m> is changing, per unit change in <m>x</m>, at that
            instant.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        Both readings matter all semester. "Slope of the tangent line" is
        the picture to keep in your head; "instantaneous rate of change" is
        how derivatives show up in every applied problem &#8212; velocity is
        the derivative of position, marginal cost is the derivative of total
        cost, and so on.
      </p>

      <example xml:id="example-derivative-from-definition">
        <title>Computing a derivative directly from the definition</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>f(x) = x^2</m>. Find <m>f'(x)</m> using
          <xref ref="def-derivative"/>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>Set up the difference quotient and simplify before taking the
          limit &#8212; plugging in <m>h=0</m> too early gives <m>0/0</m>.</p>
          <md>
            <mrow> f'(x) &amp;= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{(x+h)^2 - x^2}{h} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{x^2 + 2xh + h^2 - x^2}{h} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{2xh + h^2}{h} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \lim_{h \to 0} (2x + h) </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= 2x. </mrow>
          </md>
          <p>
            Now <m>h</m> can safely go to <m>0</m>, since it's no longer in
            a denominator. This confirms something you likely remember:
            the derivative of <m>x^2</m> is <m>2x</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <p>
        This works, but imagine doing that algebra for something like
        <m>f(x) = x^{10}</m>, or <m>f(x) = \sin(x)\cos(x)</m>. The definition
        is the <em>truth</em> of what a derivative is, but it's a slow way to
        compute one. That's what the rules below are for: they're all
        theorems you could prove from the limit definition, packaged so you
        never have to run that limit by hand again.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-differentiation-rules">
      <title>The differentiation toolkit</title>

      <p>
        These are the rules you built up over Calc I. Skim them to see what
        comes back on its own, and flag anything that doesn't &#8212; we'll
        move fast, but we'll use every one of these repeatedly for the rest
        of the semester.
      </p>

      <fact xml:id="fact-basic-rules">
        <title>Basic differentiation rules</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For differentiable functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>, and a
            constant <m>c</m>:
          </p>
          <md>
            <mrow> \text{Constant Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[c] = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Power Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[x^n] = n x^{n-1} </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Constant Multiple Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[c \, f(x)] = c \, f'(x) </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Sum/Difference Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[f(x) \pm g(x)] = f'(x) \pm g'(x) </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Product Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[f(x)g(x)] = f'(x)g(x) + f(x)g'(x) </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Quotient Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}\!\left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right] = \frac{f'(x)g(x) - f(x)g'(x)}{[g(x)]^2} </mrow>
            <mrow> \text{Chain Rule:} \qquad &amp; \frac{d}{dx}\big[f(g(x))\big] = f'(g(x))\cdot g'(x) </mrow>
          </md>
        </statement>
      </fact>

      <p>
        The Chain Rule is the one people are rustiest on, and it's the one
        we'll lean on hardest this semester (substitution, our main
        integration technique in Chapter <xref ref="ch-integrals"/>, is
        really the Chain Rule run backwards). The pattern to recognize: a
        function <term>inside</term> another function. Differentiate the
        outside function, leave the inside alone, then multiply by the
        derivative of the inside.
      </p>

      <p>
        Alongside these, you'll need the derivatives of a handful of
        specific functions on recall:
      </p>

      <fact xml:id="fact-standard-derivatives">
        <title>Standard derivatives</title>
        <statement>
          <md>
            <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}[\sin x] &amp;= \cos x
              &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[\cos x] &amp;= -\sin x </mrow>
            <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}[\tan x] &amp;= \sec^2 x
              &amp; \frac{d}{dx}[e^x] &amp;= e^x </mrow>
            <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}[\ln x] &amp;= \frac{1}{x} </mrow>
          </md>
        </statement>
      </fact>

      <example xml:id="example-product-chain">
        <title>Product Rule and Chain Rule together</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Differentiate <m>f(x) = x^2 e^{3x}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This is a product of two functions, <m>x^2</m> and <m>e^{3x}</m>,
            so start with the Product Rule. The second factor needs the
            Chain Rule on its own: the "outside" function is
            <m>e^{(\,\cdot\,)}</m> and the "inside" function is <m>3x</m>,
            whose derivative is <m>3</m>.
          </p>
          <md>
            <mrow> f'(x) &amp;= \frac{d}{dx}[x^2]\cdot e^{3x} + x^2 \cdot \frac{d}{dx}[e^{3x}] </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= 2x\,e^{3x} + x^2 \cdot 3e^{3x} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= e^{3x}(2x + 3x^2) </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= x e^{3x}(2 + 3x). </mrow>
          </md>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <example xml:id="example-quotient">
        <title>Quotient Rule</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Differentiate <m>g(x) = \dfrac{x^2 + 1}{x - 3}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <md>
            <mrow> g'(x) &amp;= \frac{(2x)(x-3) - (x^2+1)(1)}{(x-3)^2} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \frac{2x^2 - 6x - x^2 - 1}{(x-3)^2} </mrow>
            <mrow> &amp;= \frac{x^2 - 6x - 1}{(x-3)^2}. </mrow>
          </md>
        </solution>
      </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-critical-points">
      <title>Critical points</title>

      <p>
        One more piece of vocabulary we'll need immediately in the next
        chapter. Where a smooth curve has a local peak or valley, its
        tangent line is flat &#8212; slope zero. That observation is worth
        naming.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-critical-point">
        <title>Critical point</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A <term>critical point</term> of <m>f</m> is a value <m>c</m> in
            the domain of <m>f</m> where either <m>f'(c) = 0</m> or
            <m>f'(c)</m> does not exist.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        Critical points are exactly the <em>candidates</em> for where a
        function reaches a local maximum or minimum &#8212; not every
        critical point is one, but every local max or min (away from the
        endpoints of an interval) has to be one. That's the entire engine
        behind optimization, which is where we're headed next.
      </p>

      <example xml:id="example-critical-points">
        <title>Finding critical points</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Find the critical points of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>f</m> is a polynomial, so <m>f'</m> is defined everywhere;
            the only critical points come from <m>f'(x) = 0</m>.
          </p>
          <md>
            <mrow> f'(x) &amp;= 3x^2 - 3 </mrow>
            <mrow> 0 &amp;= 3x^2 - 3 </mrow>
            <mrow> 0 &amp;= 3(x^2-1) </mrow>
            <mrow> x &amp;= \pm 1. </mrow>
          </md>
          <p>
            So <m>f</m> has critical points at <m>x=-1</m> and <m>x=1</m>.
            (Sketch <m>f(x)=x^3-3x</m> if you want to see why: it has a
            local max at <m>x=-1</m> and a local min at <m>x=1</m>.)
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <p>
        With the mechanics of differentiation and the idea of a critical
        point back in hand, we're ready for Chapter <xref
        ref="ch-optimization-approximation"/>, where "find the critical
        points" becomes the key step in solving real optimization problems.
      </p>
    </subsection>

  </section>
</chapter>
